      "ts": 1790165953.294919,
      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "Ran the diff's own acceptance tests (all 6 pass) and the full repo suite (1308 tests, OK). Verified all_records/set_tiers signatures, the open-status pinning allowlist (queued/held/running via the same bus.get-vs-json-file pattern as memory_store._goal_from_bus), component-match token semantics, KIND_WEIGHTS, the pool.toml comment placement, the static HOT.md header and byte-identical idempotent rebuilds, and CLI wiring for `memory hot`/`memory compact`. Also re-ran migrate+compact against the real repo memory files: got tokens=2995 pinned=3 dropped=231 vs. the docs' claimed 2999/4/233 \u2014 a small, explainable drift from live bus task status changes since the doc was written (pinning depends on open task status), not a bug. One non-blocking gap: the 'governing decision' pointer-line feature is unreachable because no code path ever tags a decision 'governing'.",
        "verdict": "approve",
        "comments": [
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/memory_hot.py",
            "line": 108,
            "issue": "rendered()'s dropped_decisions pointer-line feature only fires for decision records tagged 'governing', but nothing in memory_store.migrate() or anywhere else in the repo (verified via grep of decisions.md and the tagging code, tags = [kind, *tag_words]) ever produces a 'governing' tag. The spec's 'pointer line memory show <id> only for governing decisions that were dropped' requirement is therefore unreachable dead code \u2014 no test exercises it either. Not acceptance-blocking (all 6 acceptance tests plus the full 1308-test suite pass, gate is green), but the promised pointer-line behavior never actually fires against real data.",
            "severity": "med"
          }
        ],
        "packet_version": "3a5ebabd0718",
        "confidence": 0.0,
        "provenance": [
          "repo"
        ]
      }
